" name=3D"q27" type=3D"radio" value=3D"b"><label for=3D"q27b"><span>Disaste=
r recovery orchestration</span></label></li>
            <li class=3D"azq-option"><input autocomplete=3D"off" id=3D"q27c=
" name=3D"q27" type=3D"radio" value=3D"c"><label for=3D"q27c"><span>Encrypt=
ing data at rest</span></label></li>
            <li class=3D"azq-option"><input autocomplete=3D"off" id=3D"q27d=
" name=3D"q27" type=3D"radio" value=3D"d"><label for=3D"q27d"><span>Deployi=
ng containers</span></label></li>
          </ul>
          <button class=3D"azq-explain-toggle" type=3D"button" aria-expande=
d=3D"true" aria-controls=3D"azq-305-practice-test-1-explanation-27" data-az=
q-explain-toggle=3D"" aria-disabled=3D"false" style=3D"display: inline-flex=
;" fdprocessedid=3D"pq7jfo">Hide explanation</button><div class=3D"azq-expl=
anation is-open" id=3D"azq-305-practice-test-1-explanation-27">
            <h3>The correct answer is Disaster recovery orchestration.</h3>
            <p>Azure Site Recovery (ASR) is designed to replicate workloads=
 running on physical and virtual machines from a primary site to a secondar=
y location. In the event of an outage, you can fail over to the secondary s=
ite and continue operations with minimal disruption. ASR supports replicati=
on across Azure regions as well as from on-premises to Azure, offering flex=
ible DR options. It also automates recovery plans and testing to ensure bus=
iness continuity. ASR helps meet compliance and availability requirements f=
or critical workloads.</p>
